# What may be uploaded to a deploy. This file is a boundary, not a speed-up.
#
# bin/publish.mjs opens by saying the deploy "has nothing to leak, because it
# never holds the collection in the first place" — items/ is gitignored, Vercel
# builds from a tree that has never contained it, and everything the vault shows
# arrives at runtime from R2. That is the whole security argument for the
# architecture.
#
# It was not true of a CLI deploy. `vercel --prod` computed a 498MB upload from
# a repo whose non-ignored content is 2.6MB: with no .vercelignore present the
# CLI did not apply .gitignore, so the collection, the built site and the capture
# media were all being packed up and sent. The upload then failed on its own
# size, which is the only reason it was noticed — the deploys that succeeded
# 19 hours earlier did the same thing and simply fit.
#
# So the rule that keeps the collection off the network is written down HERE,
# where the CLI actually reads it, and not inferred from .gitignore.
#
# Keep the two in step. Anything added to .gitignore because it is third-party
# source, capture media or built output belongs in this list too.

# ── The collection. Paid third-party source; never leaves this machine ───────
items/
archives/

# ── Generated media and built output, reproducible from source ───────────────
out/
site/
site-fixtures/
fixtures/**/_next/
fixtures/**/.next/
fixtures/**/bundle.js

# ── Dependencies and build caches (also Vercel defaults; listed to be explicit)
node_modules/
.pnpm-store/
.next/
web/.next/
web/out/

# ── Local state and secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
.git/
.vercel/
.det/
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
*.log
.DS_Store
